[AIMessage → tool] create_analysis({'text': 'Available non-Wikipedia sources clarify that the "Ring of Nestor" treated in Evans (1925) and later scholarship is a specific 
Mycenaean gold signet ring, most likely identical with CMS VI, no. 277, from a rock-cut chamber tomb near Thisbê in Boeotia, not with any of the gold rings from the Griffin 
Warrior grave or other Pylos contexts. No authoritative museum or catalogue source located so far uses the English phrase "Ring of Nestor" as a modern display label or 
provides a current museum inventory number. The article should therefore (a) focus on the Boeotian signet ring as defined by Evans and subsequent glyptic studies, (b) avoid 
equating it with Griffin Warrior or Tiryns Treasure rings, and (c) be explicit that later discussions refer to CMS VI, 277 and that the modern nickname derives from Evans’s 
